{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 20, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending as a new statute reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts may face arrest or be offered a path to services instead—provided they meet strict conditions. Those eligible can avoid fines by engaging in a health-focused intervention, but failure to qualify means facing potential jail time of up to six months.\n\n***\n\nThe law empowers local authorities to direct individuals toward behavioral health support, though participation is not required by law. Police are instructed to contact a regional coordination line if someone qualifies for diversion; a mobile health team must respond within half an hour. If the team doesn’t arrive in time, officers must proceed with arrest. This tight window leaves little room for delay, raising concerns about practicality and fairness in enforcement.\n\n***\n\nSupporters argue the flexibility allows regions to tailor responses based on local capacity, while critics warn the approach may lead to inconsistent outcomes. Police leaders note that eligibility hinges on multiple factors: no outstanding warrants, no violent conduct, and no other charges. Even minor infractions can disqualify someone from receiving aid, turning what was meant as a compassionate shift into a rigid system.\n\n***\n\nThe shift reflects a broader national reckoning over how to handle substance use. While some states look to expand care, Oregon’s pivot underscores the tension between public order and individual well-being. In a country where access to care varies widely, decisions made in one city can echo across state lines. Imagine trying to eat a meal while your tongue is swollen—sometimes the simplest things become impossible when systems don’t adapt. At any age, shopping for help shouldn’t feel like a gamble.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform is complex; in this country, even simple acts like eating or shopping can feel overwhelming when systems fail to listen. Sometimes, the tongue speaks louder than the law.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005999532528221607}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.14223571121692657}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.09613944590091705}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -3.158996332786046e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.2584819495677948}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.0036547074560076}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.45470738410949707}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -7.986703712958843e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.22554735839366913}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.008500701747834682}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.26384207606315613}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.7483563423156738}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.021603383123874664}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.03223762288689613}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.06098560616374016}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.9061681032180786}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.023633332923054695}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.28369519114494324}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.005931987427175045}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.16570945084095}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -3.6000557884108275e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0022940528579056263}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.029750533401966095}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -5.2927523938706145e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -1.3828182090946939e-05}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -0.00318